# %% [markdown]
# ## 4. Calibration: nominal $\alpha$ is not a Type-I rate
#
# On simulated triples where $X \perp Y \mid Z$ holds (or fails) by construction, with
# benchmark-like autocorrelation (AR(1) innovations, $\phi \in \{0, 0.3\}$), the tests reject
# true nulls at very different rates for the same nominal $\alpha=0.05$. The benchmark's own
# residual dependence corresponds to $\phi \approx 0.3$–$0.4$, so $\phi \le 0.3$ is the
# benchmark-like slice and $\phi \ge 0.6$ a stress test. Each cell has 1,000 null and 1,000
# alternative replications at $T \in \{50, 100, 150, 400\}$, pooled over $T$ and the four
# noise regimes (Monte Carlo standard error at most 0.016 per cell before pooling).
#
# **Type-I error at nominal $\alpha=0.05$, $\phi \le 0.3$**
#
# | Mechanism | $\lvert Z\rvert$ | parcorr-gpu | gcm | wgcm | pcm | rcot | kci | splitkci | dfcit |
# |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
# | Linear | 1 | 0.09 | 0.07 | 0.06 | 0.07 | 0.06 | 0.06 | 0.04 | 0.11 |
# | Linear | 3 | 0.09 | 0.07 | 0.05 | 0.08 | 0.15 | 0.08 | 0.19 | 0.90 |
# | MLP | 1 | 0.34 | 0.07 | 0.06 | 0.10 | 0.06 | 0.06 | 0.05 | 0.08 |
# | MLP | 3 | 0.38 | 0.08 | 0.07 | 0.12 | 0.23 | 0.09 | 0.10 | 0.77 |
# | Spline | 1 | 0.52 | 0.07 | 0.07 | 0.11 | 0.06 | 0.06 | 0.05 | 0.10 |
# | Spline | 3 | 0.47 | 0.10 | 0.07 | 0.12 | 0.38 | 0.13 | 0.12 | 0.76 |
#
# **Power at nominal $\alpha=0.05$, $\phi \le 0.3$**
#
# | Mechanism | $\lvert Z\rvert$ | parcorr-gpu | gcm | wgcm | pcm | rcot | kci | splitkci | dfcit |
# |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
# | Linear | 1 | 0.94 | 0.90 | 0.85 | 0.90 | 0.87 | 0.81 | 0.55 | 0.96 |
# | Linear | 3 | 0.79 | 0.74 | 0.60 | 0.75 | 0.75 | 0.52 | 0.61 | 0.99 |
# | MLP | 1 | 0.86 | 0.84 | 0.87 | 0.93 | 0.93 | 0.90 | 0.70 | 0.94 |
# | MLP | 3 | 0.73 | 0.71 | 0.66 | 0.84 | 0.85 | 0.67 | 0.48 | 0.62 |
# | Spline | 1 | 0.71 | 0.60 | 0.68 | 0.96 | 0.87 | 0.89 | 0.74 | 0.91 |
# | Spline | 3 | 0.58 | 0.46 | 0.44 | 0.92 | 0.82 | 0.64 | 0.59 | 0.66 |
#
# **Type-I error at nominal $\alpha=0.05$ by persistence (linear triples, pooled over $\lvert Z\rvert$)**
#
# | $\phi$ | parcorr-gpu | gcm | wgcm | pcm | rcot | kci | splitkci | dfcit |
# |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
# | 0 | 0.08 | 0.06 | 0.05 | 0.07 | 0.10 | 0.06 | 0.12 | 0.50 |
# | 0.3 | 0.10 | 0.07 | 0.06 | 0.08 | 0.12 | 0.07 | 0.12 | 0.51 |
# | 0.6 | 0.20 | 0.09 | 0.16 | 0.11 | 0.22 | 0.15 | 0.15 | 0.55 |
# | 0.9 | 0.50 | 0.25 | 0.57 | 0.24 | 0.64 | 0.62 | 0.44 | 0.75 |
#
# `raft`, scored on the same calibration grid, averages 0.049 Type-I at $\phi \le 0.3$ (all
# mechanisms, at most 0.082 in any cell) and 0.212 at $\phi = 0.9$.

# %% [markdown]
# * **Benchmark-like slice ($\phi \le 0.3$).** `parcorr-gpu` is about 0.09 on linear triples
#   but rejects 0.34–0.52 of true nulls on nonlinear triples, because a linear regression
#   leaves nonlinear dependence in the residuals. `rcot` and `kci` are close to nominal with
#   one conditioning variable; with three, `rcot` rejects up to 0.38 and `kci` up to 0.13.
#   `gcm` and `wgcm` stay at or below 0.10. `dfcit` rejects 0.76–0.90 with three conditioning
#   variables.
# * **Strong persistence ($\phi \ge 0.6$).** Every test over-rejects. `gcm` and `pcm`, which
#   use a Newey–West variance, degrade least (about 0.25 at $\phi=0.9$); `wgcm`, `kci` and
#   `rcot` reach 0.57–0.64. For highly persistent residuals, none of the tests here gives
#   calibrated individual decisions.
# * **Calibration is not power.** `gcm`, `wgcm` and `pcm` detect dependence through
#   weighted covariances of regression residuals, a narrower target than full conditional
#   independence; `wgcm`'s power is the lowest on spline triples with three conditioning
#   variables.
#
# Graph rankings do **not** simply follow calibration: at short $T$ the liberal
# `parcorr-gpu` still leads F1, consistent with extra rejections adding recall that F1
# rewards. So pick the test for the loss you care about, and pick a calibrated test when
# individual CI decisions must be trusted.

# %% [markdown]
# ## 5. Threshold and compute
#
# The test and its threshold are one choice. Two findings from the benchmark:
#
# * **`parcorr-gpu` at $\alpha=0.01$ versus `wgcm` at $\alpha=0.05$**, paired on the same
#   datasets (pooled over $d \ge 10$, all noise regimes and densities): F1 **+0.036**, SHD
#   **1.02 edges lower**, median runtime **0.34 s vs 8.5 s**
#   (about 25x faster). On nonlinear null triples, `parcorr-gpu` at $\alpha=0.01$
#   still rejects 0.23–0.40 of true nulls while `wgcm` stays at or below 0.07, so the cheap
#   configuration is a graph-level choice, not a calibrated one. The SHD advantage depends on
#   $T$: it holds from $T=100$ to 400, is absent at $T=50$, falls below one edge at $T=600$ and
#   reverses slightly at $T=800$; at $d=30$ and $T \ge 600$, `wgcm` at $\alpha=0.05$ is
#   firmly ahead (all of this pooled over mechanisms). Heavy-tailed nonlinear data are the other
#   exception, with `wgcm` about 1.3 edges better. The large-$T$ reversal comes from the MLP
#   and spline mechanisms. On linear mechanisms the advantage peaks around $T=100$–150 and
#   then shrinks, but persists at every $T$: about 1.3 SHD edges at $T=50$, 3.1 at $T=100$,
#   3.2 at $T=150$, 2.0 at $T=400$ and 0.9 at $T=800$ ($d \ge 10$, all noise regimes and
#   densities). At $d=30$ it is about 0.6 edges at $T=600$ and a tie at $T=800$.
# * **Loosening $\alpha$** (0.05 to 0.20 at $d=10$) improves recall, Oset-AID and large-sample
#   effect error, but worsens SHD and short-sample effect error. Choose $\alpha$ for the final
#   use of the graph.
#
# How the threshold moves each objective ($d=10$, averaged over densities and $T \le 200$).
# For each mechanism and objective, the line follows the test that wins at $\alpha=0.05$.
# Dotted lines show LGES, a score-based search run on the same data as a reference:

